## a Claude Code session told "you're the implementer" runs: handoff role implementer
You are now implementer in quick.

handoff brief: you are implementer in quick. You and reviewer, reviewer-2 hand work to each other through handoff. The operator decides.

Listening: if your session has handoff's hooks, a hook listens for implementer when your turn ends, and you don't run wait. If not, run `handoff wait --for implementer --timeout 25m` in the background. Exit 3: run it again. Exit 4: another session already listens for implementer. After you handle what it delivers, wait again. If your session has no handoff hooks, offer to set them up for every project: `handoff settings claude --global` prints them and where they go. With the operator's OK, add them. They work once the operator starts this session again.

When something arrives, read it with `handoff show <id>`, then:
- A reply to your request: on CHANGES or QUESTION, fix or answer, then post again (`handoff post --as implementer ... --supersedes <id>`). On APPROVE, the operator decides; wait for the decision.
- A decision from the operator: act on it. Merge only on approve.
- An fyi from the operator, to every agent with a session: take it as the operator's word, as if said in this session, and act on it. Don't answer it through handoff; anything to say, say in this session.
- A note to you: answer with `handoff note --as implementer --to <role> --re <id> --body <file>`. If it has findings on work you haven't asked about, fix them, then ask for a review.

To ask for a review, commit your work on a branch of its own, not on main; you don't need to ask the operator first. Then `handoff post --as implementer --item <item> --to <reviewer> --commit <sha> --title "<title>" --body <file>`, where <reviewer> is reviewer or reviewer-2, whichever the operator gives a session; none has one yet.
To ask the operator about an item: `handoff note --as implementer --to operator --item <item> --title "<question>" --body <file>`.
Rules: never edit .handoff/ by hand. Never merge, push or deploy without the operator's decision. A claim is not an approval.

